      "text": "To determine the length of the tunnel, we need to analyze the situation step by step.\n\n1. **Convert the train's speed to miles per minute:**\n   The train is moving at 60 miles per hour. Since there are 60 minutes in an hour, we convert the speed to miles per minute:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Speed in miles per minute} = \\frac{60 \\text{ miles per hour}}{60 \\text{ minutes per hour}} = 1 \\text{ mile per minute}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the total distance traveled by the front of the train:**\n   The front of the train exits the tunnel 3 minutes after the train starts moving. Since the train is moving at 1 mile per minute, in 3 minutes the front of the train will have traveled:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance traveled by the front of the train} = 3 \\text{ minutes} \\times 1 \\text{ mile per minute} = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Set up the equation for the total distance traveled:**\n   The total distance traveled by the front of the train is the length of the train plus the length of the tunnel. We know the total distance is 1 mile (the length of the train), so we can write:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Length of the train} + \\text{Length of the tunnel} = 1 \\text{ mile}\n   \\]\n   Let \\( L \\) be the length of the tunnel. Then:\n   \\[\n   1 \\text{ mile} + L = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for the length of the tunnel:**\n   Subtract 1 mile from both sides of the equation:\n   \\[\n   L = 3 \\text{ miles} - 1 \\text{ mile} = 2 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the tunnel is \\(\\boxed{2}\\) miles.",
